# Overlay for the seven lock-archived files (2026-10-26, pre-move)

The monoforge completion sprint repaired files that live INSIDE
`horcrux/monoforge-0.9.0-lock.tar.gz` — six of them ride the unified diff
at the bottom of this file (the docs `_config.yml`, its layout, and
`freestruct.config.yml`, both stamped-side and template-side; the git
paths keep their mount prefix so it applies tree-top):

- `monoforge-staging/docs/_config.yml` + `_layouts/default.html` +
  `freestruct.config.yml`
- `monoforge-staging/templates/docs/_config.yml` + `_layouts/default.html`
- `monoforge-staging/templates/docs/freestruct.config.yml`

(`docs/verbs.md`, `docs/packs.md`, and the `site/` tree land as normal
new files — the ferry never deletes extra files, so they survive it;
only files THE ARCHIVE CONTAINS get reassembled back to archive bytes.)

The gistory ferry law — *the mount tool tree matches its lock* — reassembles
files that ride in the lock archive back to their ARCHIVE bytes on every
interleaved ferry run, so landing the fixed on-disk bytes was not durable: the
system worked, the lane was wrong.

That repair canonically belongs **upstream in dhaupin/monoforge**, which needs
a re-lock to converge (a lock only receipts a green tree). Until move day:
this patch carries the fixed bytes durably in the mount's horcrux slot — the
tar never deletes extra files, so a receipt under `horcrux/` is ferry-safe.

Convergence at move day (needs the first write grant on dhaupin/monoforge):
1. `git apply monoforge-staging/horcrux/lock-archive-overlay.patch
   (git blob d80cfbf8f3fe1458dff268dc096a547ef910c845)`
2. rerun the self-test battery (stamp/doctor/lock refusals stay green)
3. `bin/forge.mjs lock` re-cut of `monoforge-0.9.0-lock.tar.gz` (bump the
   sha record in the lock record file), commit upstream
4. ingest the re-lock into gistory (update the ferry's sha pin), then the
   mount tool tree IS the fixed bytes and this patch retires (delete it in
   the same commit the ferry pin moves).

